<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duchy of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/661">661</a>–<a href="/years/1137">1137</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Byzantine Duchy of Naples</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> was a Byzantine province on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a> that became a largely independent state under its own dukes in the 8th century, while nominally recognising the emperor. It resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and Arab raiders. Amalfi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> broke away from it in the 9th century, and the Normans under Roger II absorbed it in 1137.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> began as a Byzantine province, constituted in the seventh century in the lands roughly corresponding to the present province of Naples that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> had not conquered in their invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the sixth century. It extended over the Vesuvius zone, the Sorrento Peninsula, the Phlegraean area, the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> and Procida, Nola and parts of the present province of Caserta. In 661 Naples obtained from the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> the right to be ruled by a local duke, Basil, whose subjection to the emperor soon became merely nominal. Greek was the official language, though the population spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and the duchy coined money with the emperor's effigy and Greek inscriptions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 763 Duke Stephen II switched his allegiance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, placing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> under papal suzerainty. Around the beginning of the ninth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> replaced Greek in official use and Saint Januarius replaced the emperor on the coins. In 813 Duke Anthimus could ignore Emperor Leo V's call for the fleet to fight Saracen pirates off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>; only Amalfi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> sent contingents. In 840 Duke Sergius I made the succession hereditary, and Naples was thereafter de facto independent. Gaeta, Amalfi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> took their independence from Naples in the early ninth century.</p><p>The city was mainly a military centre, ruled by an aristocracy of warriors and landowners, with a population of between thirty and thirty-five thousand. Its fleet took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ostia">Battle of Ostia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 849, yet in 836 it asked the Arabs for support against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> siege from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. Dukes such as Gregory IV and John II fought at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 915. In 1027 Duke Sergius IV gave the county of Aversa to Norman mercenaries led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainulf-drengot">Rainulf Drengot</a>, which began a process that eventually ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>'s independence. In 1137 Duke Sergius VII surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The duchy lasted more than five centuries in the Early and High Middle Ages. It outlasted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> principalities of Capua, Salerno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and the Greek duchies of Amalfi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>. Under the Normans the city was administered by a palatine count, with little independence left to the Neapolitan patriciate, and commerce was left mainly to merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Norman buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> were mainly castles, walls and fortified gates, among them Castel Capuano and Castel dell'Ovo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duchy of Naples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sorrento, an old Roman town on the peninsula south of the Bay of Naples, was part of the <b>Byzantine Duchy of Naples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sorrento">Duchy of Sorrento</a></li><li><blockquote>Amalfi was a small fortified town on the steep coast of the Sorrento peninsula, part of the <b>Byzantine Duchy of Naples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-amalfi">Republic of Amalfi</a></li></ul></section>
